Notes on the judgment calls:

- **Units 2 & 8** (identical text): the only candidate that resolves both fused boundaries without severing a clause fragment (`but with Brackets set to true` and `and allows...` are not independent-clause splits) or leaving any sentence pair still fused is c14 — a clean one-sentence-per-line split with no internal sub-splits.
- **Unit 5**: `which` is the checker's algorithmic rightward-scan hit, but no candidate offers a clean split there without severing the inline-code span `MakePair(int, string)`. Between the two clause-safe options, c07 (sentence-only split) and c09 (also splits at the valid independent-clause `and`), I chose c09 since a real boundary is available and the line is well over the long threshold — but both are legitimate, so both are accepted.
- **Units 3 & 4**: these are column-wrapped `wrap`+`fused` windows whose sentence continues past the shown lines, so per the rule I only rejoin the severed word and split at the one sentence boundary I can see (c01) — not forcing further splits into text I can't verify.
